Output~2025-04-24 16:36:04.818759: [RequestOutput(request_id=147, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Write a javascript function which calculates the square root of a number without any libraries. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 9842, 264, 36810, 734, 902, 48517, 279, 9518, 3789, 315, 264, 1396, 2085, 904, 20797, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a JavaScript function that calculates the square root of a number without using any libraries:\n```\nfunction sqrt(x) {\n  if (x < 0) {\n    throw new Error("Cannot calculate square root of a negative number");\n  }\n  if (x === 0 || x === 1) {\n    return x;\n  }\n\n  let guess = x / 2;\n  let tolerance = 0.00001;\n\n  while (true) {\n    let betterGuess = ((guess + x / guess) / 2);\n    if (Math.abs(guess - betterGuess) < tolerance) {\n      return betterGuess;\n    }\n    guess = betterGuess;\n  }\n}\n```\nThis function uses the Babylonian method for calculating square roots, which is an iterative method that starts with an initial guess and repeatedly improves the guess until it converges to the correct value.\n\nHere\'s an explanation of how the function works:\n\n1. If the input `x` is negative, we throw an error because square roots of negative numbers are not real numbers.\n2. If `x` is 0 or 1, we return `x` immediately because the square root of 0 is 0 and the square root of 1 is 1.\n3. We start with an initial guess for the square root, which is `x / 2`. This is a reasonable starting point because the square root of a number is always between 0 and the number itself.\n4. We then enter a loop that repeatedly improves the guess using the Babylonian method. The loop continues until the difference between the current guess and the previous guess is less than a small tolerance value (`tolerance`).\n5. In each iteration, we calculate a new guess using the formula `(guess + x / guess) / 2`. This formula is based on the fact that the square root of `x` is the value `y` such that `y^2 = x`. We can use this fact to improve our guess by taking the average of `guess` and `x / guess`, which is a better estimate of `y`.\n6. Once the loop converges, we return the final guess as the square root of `x`.\n\nYou can test this function with different inputs to see that it produces accurate results. For example:\n```\nconsole.log(sqrt(4)); // Output: 2\nconsole.log(sqrt(9)); // Output: 3\nconsole.log(sqrt(16)); // Output: 4\nconsole.log', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 13210, 734, 430, 48517, 279, 9518, 3789, 315, 264, 1396, 2085, 1701, 904, 20797, 512, 14196, 4077, 1723, 18430, 2120, 8, 341, 220, 422, 320, 87, 366, 220, 15, 8, 341, 262, 2571, 502, 4703, 446, 17902, 11294, 9518, 3789, 315, 264, 8389, 1396, 803, 220, 457, 220, 422, 320, 87, 2093, 220, 15, 1393, 865, 2093, 220, 16, 8, 341, 262, 471, 865, 280, 220, 557, 220, 1095, 8101, 284, 865, 611, 220, 17, 280, 220, 1095, 25065, 284, 220, 15, 13, 931, 1721, 401, 220, 1418, 320, 1904, 8, 341, 262, 1095, 2731, 46830, 284, 1819, 52851, 489, 865, 611, 8101, 8, 611, 220, 17, 317, 262, 422, 320, 8991, 14914, 99402, 482, 2731, 46830, 8, 366, 25065, 8, 341, 415, 471, 2731, 46830, 280, 262, 457, 262, 8101, 284, 2731, 46830, 280, 220, 457, 534, 14196, 4077, 2028, 734, 5829, 279, 64678, 1122, 1749, 369, 38714, 9518, 20282, 11, 902, 374, 459, 87975, 1749, 430, 8638, 449, 459, 2926, 8101, 323, 19352, 36050, 279, 8101, 3156, 433, 19873, 4282, 311, 279, 4495, 907, 382, 8586, 596, 459, 16540, 315, 1268, 279, 734, 4375, 1473, 16, 13