Output~2025-04-24 16:36:08.219289: [RequestOutput(request_id=148, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Reverse a linked list using haskel. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 25263, 264, 10815, 1160, 1701, 706, 18126, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a possible implementation of reversing a linked list in Haskell:\n```\n-- Define a data type for a linked list node\ndata LinkedList a = Nil | Cons a (LinkedList a)\n\n-- Function to reverse a linked list\nreverse :: LinkedList a -> LinkedList a\nreverse Nil = Nil\nreverse (Cons x xs) = reverse xs `Cons` x\n\n-- Example usage:\nmain :: IO ()\nmain = do\n  let list = Cons 1 (Cons 2 (Cons 3 Nil))\n  print $ reverse list\n```\nThis code defines a data type `LinkedList a` to represent a linked list of elements of type `a`. The `Cons` constructor creates a new node with an element and a reference to the rest of the list, and `Nil` represents the empty list.\n\nThe `reverse` function takes a linked list as input and returns a new linked list with the elements in reverse order. It does this by recursively calling itself on the rest of the list (`xs`) and then consing the current element (`x`) onto the result.\n\nIn the example usage, we create a linked list `list` with the elements 1, 2, and 3, and then print the result of reversing it using the `reverse` function. The output should be the linked list `Cons 3 (Cons 2 (Cons 1 Nil))`, which is the original list with the elements in reverse order.', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 3284, 8292, 315, 76283, 264, 10815, 1160, 304, 63276, 512, 14196, 4077, 313, 19127, 264, 828, 955, 369, 264, 10815, 1160, 2494, 198, 695, 23767, 264, 284, 33374, 765, 7440, 264, 320, 35015, 264, 696, 313, 5830, 311, 10134, 264, 10815, 1160, 198, 26992, 3588, 23767, 264, 1492, 23767, 264, 198, 26992, 33374, 284, 33374, 198, 26992, 320, 15577, 865, 12209, 8, 284, 10134, 12209, 1595, 15577, 63, 865, 271, 313, 13688, 10648, 512, 3902, 3588, 6565, 12957, 3902, 284, 656, 198, 220, 1095, 1160, 284, 7440, 220, 16, 320, 15577, 220, 17, 320, 15577, 220, 18, 33374, 1192, 220, 1194, 400, 10134, 1160, 198, 14196, 4077, 2028, 2082, 19170, 264, 828, 955, 1595, 35015, 264, 63, 311, 4097, 264, 10815, 1160, 315, 5540, 315, 955, 1595, 64, 29687, 578, 1595, 15577, 63, 4797, 11705, 264, 502, 2494, 449, 459, 2449, 323, 264, 5905, 311, 279, 2800, 315, 279, 1160, 11, 323, 1595, 19604, 63, 11105, 279, 4384, 1160, 382, 791, 1595, 26992, 63, 734, 5097, 264, 10815, 1160, 439, 1988, 323, 4780, 264, 502, 10815, 1160, 449, 279, 5540, 304, 10134, 2015, 13, 1102, 1587, 420, 555, 53947, 8260, 5196, 389, 279, 2800, 315, 279, 1160, 29754, 19072, 33981, 323, 1243, 1615, 287, 279, 1510, 2449, 29754, 87, 33981, 8800, 279, 1121, 382, 644, 279, 3187, 10648, 11, 584, 1893, 264, 10815, 1160, 1595, 1638, 63, 449, 279, 5540, 220, 16, 11, 220, 17, 11, 323, 220, 18, 11, 323, 1243, 1194, 279, 1121, 315, 76283, 433, 1701, 279, 1595, 26992, 63, 734, 13, 578, 2612, 1288, 387, 279, 10815, 1160, 1595, 15577, 220, 18, 320, 15577, 220, 17, 320, 15577, 220, 16, 33374, 595, 7964, 902, 374, 279, 4113, 1160, 449, 279, 5540, 304, 10134, 2015, 13, 128009], cumulative_logprob=None, logprobs=None, finish_reason=stop, stop_reason=None)], finished=True, metrics=None, lora_request=None, num_cached_tokens=None, multi_modal_placeholders={})]
