Output~2025-04-24 16:43:35.446410: [RequestOutput(request_id=345, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Generate a function to sort a list of strings alphabetically ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 20400, 264, 734, 311, 3460, 264, 1160, 315, 9246, 28890, 2740, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a Python function that sorts a list of strings alphabetically:\n```\ndef sort_strings(lst):\n    return sorted(lst)\n```\nThis function uses the built-in `sorted()` function in Python, which returns a new sorted list from the elements of any sequence. In this case, it takes a list of strings as input and returns a new list with the strings in alphabetical order.\n\nHere\'s an example usage:\n```\nstrings = ["hello", "world", "abc", "def", "ghi"]\nsorted_strings = sort_strings(strings)\nprint(sorted_strings)  # Output: ["abc", "def", "ghi", "hello", "world"]\n```\nNote that this function does not modify the original list, but returns a new sorted list. If you want to sort the original list in place, you can use the `sort()` method:\n```\ndef sort_strings_in_place(lst):\n    lst.sort()\n```\nThis function modifies the original list by sorting it in place.', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 13325, 734, 430, 21522, 264, 1160, 315, 9246, 28890, 2740, 512, 14196, 4077, 755, 3460, 34600, 47146, 997, 262, 471, 10839, 47146, 340, 14196, 4077, 2028, 734, 5829, 279, 5918, 3502, 1595, 29398, 55358, 734, 304, 13325, 11, 902, 4780, 264, 502, 10839, 1160, 505, 279, 5540, 3