r implementing blur reduction techniques such as edge detection, sharpening, or anti-aliasing to improve the image quality.\n3. **Sketch preprocessing**: The program might benefit from preprocessing the sketch input to enhance its quality before generating the image. This could include techniques such as noise reduction, thresholding, or edge detection to improve the clarity and definition of the sketch.\n4. **Algorithm optimization**: The program's algorithm might be the primary cause of the low-quality output. Consider optimizing the algorithm to better handle the complexity of the sketch inputs. This could involve adjusting the program's parameters, using more advanced algorithms, or incorporating machine learning techniques to improve the image generation process.\n5. **Comparison with original sketch**: To better understand the issue, compare the generated image with the original sketch to identify specific areas where the program is struggling. This could help pinpoint the root cause of the problem and guide the development of targeted improvements.\n6. **Testing with different inputs**: Test the program with a variety of sketch inputs to see if the issue is specific to certain types of sketches or if it's a general problem. This could help identify any patterns or correlations between the input and output quality.\n7. **Image post-processing**: Consider applying post-processing techniques to the generated images to enhance their quality. This could include techniques such as color correction, contrast adjustment, or texture enhancement to improve the overall appearance of the images.\n\nBy addressing these potential issues, you should be able to improve the execution of the program and generate higher-quality images from sketch inputs.", token_ids=[29815, 389, 279, 17074, 3135, 11, 433, 8111, 430, 279, 2068, 374, 20558, 311, 30357, 7068, 1579, 22867, 5448, 505, 26610, 11374, 13, 2057, 7417, 279, 11572, 315, 279, 2068, 11, 358, 4284, 279, 2768, 1473, 16, 13, 3