Output~2025-04-24 16:59:21.880484: [RequestOutput(request_id=726, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Generate a pseudocode for sorting an array. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 20400, 264, 51743, 44788, 369, 29373, 459, 1358, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="Here is a pseudocode for sorting an array using the Bubble Sort algorithm:\n\n```\nProcedure BubbleSort(arr)\n    n = length of arr\n    for i = 1 to n-1\n        for j = 1 to n-i\n            if arr[j-1] > arr[j]\n                swap arr[j-1] and arr[j]\n    end for\nend procedure\n```\n\nThis pseudocode sorts the array in ascending order. The outer loop iterates `n-1` times, and the inner loop iterates `n-i` times. In each iteration of the inner loop, it compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. This process continues until the entire array is sorted.\n\nNote: This is a simple and inefficient sorting algorithm, and it's not recommended for large datasets. For larger datasets, more efficient algorithms like QuickSort, MergeSort, or HeapSort are recommended.", token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 51743, 44788, 369, 29373, 459, 1358, 1701, 279, 54424, 16347, 12384, 1473, 14196, 4077, 34255, 54424, 10442, 11179, 340, 262, 308, 284, 3160, 315, 2961, 198, 262, 369, 602, 284, 220, 16, 311, 308, 12, 16, 198, 286, 369, 503, 284, 220, 16, 311, 308, 18064, 198, 310, 422, 2961, 3894, 12, 16, 60, 871, 2961, 3894, 933, 394, 14626, 2961, 3894, 12, 16, 60, 323, 2961, 3894, 933,