Output~2025-04-24 17:01:04.916333: [RequestOutput(request_id=781, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Generate an algorithm for a sorting machine. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 20400, 459, 12384, 369, 264, 29373, 5780, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a simple algorithm for a sorting machine:\n\n**Sorting Algorithm: Bubble Sort**\n\n**Input:** An array of n elements of comparable values (e.g., integers, strings, etc.)\n\n**Output:** A sorted array of the same elements in ascending order\n\n**Step-by-Step Instructions:**\n\n1. **Initialization:**\n\t* Set a flag `swapped` to `false` to track if any swaps were made in the current iteration.\n\t* Set `i` to 0, which will be used as the index for the outer loop.\n2. **Outer Loop:**\n\t* Repeat the following steps until `i` is less than `n-1`, where `n` is the length of the array.\n\t\t+ Set `j` to `i+1`, which will be used as the index for the inner loop.\n\t\t+ Compare the elements at indices `i` and `j`.\n\t\t+ If the elements are in the correct order (i.e., the element at index `i` is less than or equal to the element at index `j`), set `j` to `j+1` and continue to the next iteration.\n\t\t+ If the elements are not in the correct order, swap the elements at indices `i` and `j`.\n\t\t+ Set `swapped` to `true`.\n3. **Repeat the outer loop until no more swaps are made:**\n\t* If `swapped` is still `false` after the outer loop, the array is already sorted, and the algorithm can terminate.\n\t* Otherwise, repeat the outer loop until `swapped` is `false`.\n4. **Return the sorted array:**\n\t* The array is now sorted in ascending order.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose we have the following array: `[5, 2, 8, 3, 1, 6, 4]`\n\nAfter the first iteration of the outer loop, the array becomes: `[2, 5, 8, 3, 1, 6, 4]`\n\nAfter the second iteration, the array becomes: `[2, 3, 5, 1, 6, 4, 8]`\n\nAfter the third iteration, the array becomes: `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8]`\n\nThe algorithm terminates since no more swaps were made, and the array is now sorted in ascending order.\n\n**', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 4382, 12384, 369, 264, 29373, 5780, 1473, 334, 72781, 41425, 25, 54424, 16347, 57277, 334, 2566, 68063, 1556, 1358, 315, 308, 5540, 315, 30139, 2819, 320, 68, 1326, 2637, 26864, 11, 9246, 11, 5099, 9456, 334, 5207, 68063, 362, 10839, 1358, 315, 279, 1890, 5540, 304, 36488, 2015, 271, 334, 8468, 14656, 12, 8468, 39397, 25, 57277, 16, 13, 3146, 63028, 25, 1035, 197, 9, 2638, 264, 5292, 1595, 2332, 5795, 63, 311, 1595, 3934, 63, 311, 3839, 422, 904, 74251, 1051, 1903, 304, 279, 1510, 20140, 627, 197, 9, 2638, 1595, 72, 63, 311, 220, 15, 11, 902, 690, 387, 1511, 439, 279, 1963, 369, 279, 16335, 6471, 627, 17, 13, 3146, 52422, 22070, 25, 1035, 197, 9, 45901, 279, 2768, 7504, 3156, 1595, 72, 63, 374, 2753, 1109, 1595, 77, 12, 16, 7964, 1405, 1595, 77, 63, 374, 279, 3160, 315, 279, 1358, 627, 197, 197, 10, 2638, 1595, 73, 63, 311, 1595, 72, 10, 16, 7964, 902, 690, 387, 1511, 439, 279, 1963, 369, 279, 9358, 6471, 627, 197, 197, 10, 24702, 279, 5540, 520, 15285, 1595, 72, 63, 323, 1595, 73, 19154, 197, 197, 10, 1442, 279, 5540, 527, 304, 279, 4495, 2015, 320, 72, 1770, 2637, 279, 2449, 520, 1963, 1595, 72, 63, 374, 2753, 1109, 477, 6273, 311, 279, 2449, 520, 1963, 1595, 73, 63, 705, 743, 1595, 73, 63, 311, 1595, 73, 10, 16, 63, 323, 3136, 311, 279, 1828, 20140, 627, 197, 197, 10, 1442, 279, 5540, 527, 539, 304, 279, 4495, 2015, 11, 14626, 279, 5540, 520, 15285, 1595, 72, 63, 