Output~2025-04-24 17:03:39.765995: [RequestOutput(request_id=839, prompt='<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{"Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Explain Schrodinger\'s wave equation. ### Response:"}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n', prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 5018, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 83017, 5124, 24409, 5248, 596, 12330, 24524, 13, 17010, 6075, 3047, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="Schrödinger's wave equation is a mathematical formulation of the time-dependent and time-independent Schrödinger equations in quantum mechanics. It is a central concept in quantum mechanics, which was introduced by Austrian physicist Erwin Schrödinger in 1926.\n\nThe time-dependent Schrödinger equation is a partial differential equation that describes the time-evolution of a quantum system. It is given by:\n\niℏ(∂ψ/∂t) = Hψ\n\nwhere:\n\n* ψ is the wave function of the system, which describes the quantum state of the system.\n* t is time.\n* i is the imaginary unit, which is defined as the square root of -1.\n* ℏ is the reduced Planck constant, which is a fundamental constant of nature.\n* H is the Hamiltonian operator, which represents the total energy of the system.\n\nThe time-independent Schrödinger equation is a partial differential equation that describes the stationary states of a quantum system. It is given by:\n\nHψ = Eψ\n\nwhere:\n\n* ψ is the wave function of the system, which describes the quantum state of the system.\n* E is the energy of the system.\n* H is the Hamiltonian operator, which represents the total energy of the system.\n\nThe Schrödinger equation is a fundamental equation in quantum mechanics, and it is used to describe the behavior of particles at the atomic and subatomic level. It is a crucial tool for understanding the properties of atoms, molecules, and other quantum systems.\n\nIn essence, the Schrödinger equation describes how the wave function of a quantum system changes over time, and how it is affected by the potential energy of the system. The equation is a mathematical representation of the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics, and it is used to predict the behavior of quantum systems with high accuracy.\n\nIn summary, the Schrödinger equation is a fundamental equation in quantum mechanics that describes the time-evolution of a quantum system and the stationary states of a quantum system. It is a crucial tool for understanding the behavior of particles at the atomic and subatomic level, and it has been widely used to make accurate predictions about the behavior of quantum systems.", token_ids=[50, 17207, 3029, 67, 5248, 596, 12330, 24524, 374, 264, 37072, 55986, 315, 279, 892, 43918, 323, 892, 98885, 5124, 81, 3029, 67, 5248, 39006, 304, 31228, 30126, 13, 1102, 374, 264, 8792, 7434, 304, 31228, 30126, 11, 902, 574, 11784, 555, 58069, 83323, 9939, 7678, 5124, 81, 3029, 67, 5248, 304, 220, 5926, 21, 382, 791, 892, 43918, 5124, 81, 3029, 67, 5248, 24524, 374, 264, 7276, 41264, 24524, 430, 16964, 279, 892, 91345, 3294, 315, 264, 31228, 1887, 13, 1102, 374, 2728, 555, 1473, 72, 15284, 237, 7, 22447, 224, 101468, 14, 22447, 224, 83, 8, 284, 473, 101468, 271, 2940, 1473, 9, 112091, 374, 279, 12330, 734, 315, 279, 1887, 11, 902, 16964, 279, 31228, 1614, 315, 279, 1887, 627, 9, 259, 374, 892, 627, 9, 602, 374, 279, 51052, 5089, 11, 902, 374, 4613, 439, 279, 9518, 3789, 315, 482, 16, 627, 9, 29753, 237, 374, 279, 11293, 9878, 377, 6926, 11, 902, 374, 264, 16188, 6926, 315, 7138, 627, 9, 473, 374, 279, 24051, 1122, 5793, 11, 902, 11105, 279, 2860, 4907, 315, 279, 1887, 382, 791, 892, 98885, 5124, 81, 3029, 67, 5248, 24524, 374, 