node1, node2);\n  rightResult = findFirstCommonAncestor(root.right, node1, node2);\n\n  if (leftResult != null) {\n    return leftResult;\n  } else if (rightResult != null) {\n    return rightResult;\n  } else {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n```\n**Time complexity:** O(h), where h is the height of the binary tree.\n\n**Space complexity:** O(1), since we only use a constant amount of space to store the recursive function calls.\n\nThis algorithm works by traversing the binary tree in a depth-first manner, starting from the root node. It compares the current node's value with the values of the two nodes we're looking for, and recursively calls itself on the left and right subtrees if necessary. The first node that returns a non-null result is the first common ancestor of the two nodes. If both subtrees return null, it means that the two nodes are not in the same subtree, and we return null as the first common ancestor.", token_ids=[8586, 374, 459, 12384, 311, 1505, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 315, 1403, 7954, 304, 264, 8026, 5021, 1473, 334, 28947, 25, 57277, 16, 13, 5256, 520, 279, 3789, 2494, 315, 279, 8026, 5021, 627, 17, 13, 24702, 279, 1510, 2494, 596, 907, 449, 279, 2819, 315, 279, 1403, 7954, 499, 2351, 3411, 369, 320, 1169, 596, 1650, 1124, 1595, 3593, 16, 63, 323, 1595, 3593, 17, 63, 4390, 18, 13, 1442, 279, 1510, 2494, 596, 907, 374, 6273, 311, 3060, 1595, 3593, 16, 63, 477, 1595, 3593, 17, 7964, 471, 279, 1510, 2494, 439, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 627, 19, 13, 1442, 279, 1510, 2494, 596, 907, 374, 539, 6273, 311, 3060, 1595, 3593, 16, 63, 477, 1595, 3593, 17, 7964, 53947, 1650, 279, 12384, 389, 279, 2163, 54564, 320, 333, 433, 6866, 8, 323, 279, 1314, 54564, 320, 333, 433, 6866, 4390, 20, 13, 1442, 279, 2163, 54564, 4780, 264, 2536, 61441, 1121, 11, 471, 430, 1121, 439, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 627, 21, 13, 1442, 279, 1314, 54564, 4780, 264, 2536, 61441, 1121, 11, 471, 430, 1121, 439, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 627, 22, 13, 1442, 2225, 42129, 8016, 471, 854, 11, 471, 85