Output~2025-04-24 17:33:29.514372: [RequestOutput(request_id=1595, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: What is the difference between a static method and an instance method? ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 3639, 374, 279, 6811, 1990, 264, 1118, 1749, 323, 459, 2937, 1749, 30, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="In object-oriented programming, a method is a block of code that can be executed within an object. There are two types of methods: static methods and instance methods.\n\n**Static Methods:**\n\nA static method is a method that belongs to a class, rather than an instance of the class. This means that a static method can be called without creating an instance of the class. Static methods are essentially global functions that are associated with a class, rather than an object.\n\nHere are some key characteristics of static methods:\n\n* Can be called without creating an instance of the class\n* Belong to the class, not an instance of the class\n* Share the same copy of the method in memory, regardless of the number of instances created\n* Do not have access to the `this` keyword, as they are not bound to a specific object\n* Cannot be overridden by subclasses\n\n**Instance Methods:**\n\nAn instance method, on the other hand, is a method that belongs to an instance of a class. This means that an instance method can only be called on an object that has been created from the class. Instance methods are bound to a specific object and can access and modify the object's state.\n\nHere are some key characteristics of instance methods:\n\n* Can only be called on an instance of the class\n* Belong to an instance of the class, not the class itself\n* Each instance has its own copy of the method in memory\n* Have access to the `this` keyword, which refers to the object on which the method is being called\n* Can be overridden by subclasses\n\nIn summary, the main difference between a static method and an instance method is that a static method is associated with a class, while an instance method is associated with an instance of a class. Static methods can be called without creating an instance, while instance methods require an instance to be created before they can be called.", token_ids=[644, 1665, 36185, 15840, 11, 264, 1749, 374, 264, 2565, 315, 2082, 430, 649, 387, 16070, 2949, 459, 1665, 13, 2684, 527, 1403, 4595, 315, 5528, 25, 1118, 5528, 323, 2937, 5528, 382, 334, 11952, 19331, 25, 57277, 32, 1118, 1749, 374, 264, 1749, 430, 17623, 311, 264, 538, 11, 4856, 1109, 459, 2937, 315, 279, 538, 13, 1115, 3445, 430, 264, 1118, 1749, 649, 387, 2663, 2085, 6968, 459, 2937, 315, 279, 538, 13, 23977, 5528, 527, 16168, 3728, 5865, 430, 527, 5938, 449, 264, 538, 11, 4856, 1109, 459, 1665, 382, 8586, 527, 1063, 1401, 17910, 315, 1118, 5528, 1473, 9, 3053, 387, 2663, 2085, 6968, 459, 2937, 315, 279, 538, 198, 9, 7984, 647, 311, 279, 538, 11, 539, 459, 2937, 315, 279, 538, 198, 9, 12037, 279, 1890, 3048, 315, 279, 1749, 304, 5044, 11, 15851, 315, 279, 1396, 315, 13422, 3549, 198, 9, 3234, 539, 617, 2680, 311, 279, 1595, 576, 63, 16570, 11, 439, 814, 527, 539, 6965, 311, 264, 3230, 1665, 198, 9, 35755, 387, 52556, 555, 69283, 271, 334, 2584, 19331, 25, 57277, 2127, 2937, 1749, 11, 389, 279, 1023, 1450, 11, 374, 264, 1749, 430, 17623, 311, 459, 2937, 315, 264, 538, 13, 1115, 3445, 430, 459, 2937, 1749, 649, 1193, 387, 2663, 389, 459, 1665, 430, 706, 1027, 3549, 505, 279, 538, 13, 19840, 5528, 527, 6965, 311, 264, 3230, 1665, 