Output~2025-04-24 17:40:23.384726: [RequestOutput(request_id=1719, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Explain the theory of gravity. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 83017, 279, 10334, 315, 24128, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='The theory of gravity is a fundamental concept in physics that describes the force that attracts two objects with mass towards each other. The theory was first described by Sir Isaac Newton in his groundbreaking work "Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica" in 1687.\n\nAccording to Newton\'s law of universal gravitation, every point mass attracts every other point mass by a force acting along the line intersecting both points. The force of gravity is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. This is often expressed mathematically as:\n\nF = G \\* (m1 \\* m2) / r^2\n\nWhere F is the force of gravity, G is the gravitational constant, m1 and m2 are the masses of the two objects, and r is the distance between their centers.\n\nIn simpler terms, the force of gravity is what keeps us on the ground, what makes things fall towards the ground, and what holds planets in orbit around their stars. It is a universal force that affects everything with mass, from the smallest subatomic particles to the largest galaxies.\n\nHowever, Newton\'s theory of gravity was later refined by Albert Einstein\'s theory of general relativity, which describes gravity not as a force between objects, but as the curvature of spacetime caused by the presence of mass and energy. According to general relativity, the curvature of spacetime around a massive object such as a star or a black hole is what we experience as the force of gravity.\n\nIn summary, the theory of gravity is a fundamental concept in physics that describes the force that attracts two objects with mass towards each other, and is a key component of our understanding of the natural world.', token_ids=[791, 10334, 315, 24128, 374, 264, 16188, 7434, 304, 22027, 430, 16964, 279, 5457, 430, 61191, 1403, 6302, 449, 3148, 7119, 1855, 1023, 13, 578, 10334, 574, 1176, 7633, 555, 17177, 42608, 21324, 304, 813, 64955, 990, 330, 30690, 11597, 72, 9371, 18955, 285, 45629, 689, 72140, 64, 1, 304, 220, 8953, 22, 382, 11439, 311, 21324, 596, 2383, 315, 20789, 29059, 7709, 11, 1475, 1486, 3148, 61191, 1475, 1023, 1486, 3148, 555, 264, 5457, 15718, 3235, 279, 1584, 32896, 287, 2225, 3585, 13, 578, 5457, 315, 24128, 374, 55272, 311, 279, 2027, 315, 279, 1403, 32738, 323, 65683, 989, 55272, 311, 279, 9518, 315, 279, 6138, 1990, 1124, 13, 1115, 374, 3629, 13605, 7033, 336, 7167, 439, 1473, 37, 284, 480, 1144, 9, 320, 76, 16, 1144, 9, 296, 17, 8, 611, 436, 61, 17, 271, 9241, 435, 374, 279, 5457, 315, 24128, 11, 480, 374, 279, 71019, 6926, 11, 296, 16, 323, 296, 17, 527, 279, 32738, 315, 279, 1403, 6302, 11, 323, 436, 374, 279, 6138, 1990, 872, 19169, 382, 644, 35388, 3878, 11, 279, 5457, 315, 24128, 374, 1148, 13912, 603, 389, 279, 5015, 11, 1148, 3727, 2574, 4498, 7119, 279, 5015, 11, 323, 1148, 10187, 33975, 304, 27605, 2212, 872, 9958, 13, 1102, 374, 264, 20789, 5457, 430, 22223, 4395, 449, 3148, 11, 505, 279, 25655, 1207, 6756, 19252, 311, 279, 7928, 66017, 382, 11458, 11, 21324, 596, 10334, 315, 24128, 574, 3010, 38291, 555, 17971, 55152, 596, 10334, 315, 4689, 1375, 44515, 11, 902, 16964, 24128, 539, 439, 264, 5457, 1990, 6302, 11, 719, 439, 279, 83233, 315, 100108, 4199, 9057, 555, 