Output~2025-04-24 17:46:24.453289: [RequestOutput(request_id=1862, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: What is the difference between a shallow copy and deep copy in Python? ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 3639, 374, 279, 6811, 1990, 264, 26682, 3048, 323, 5655, 3048, 304, 13325, 30, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="In Python, when you want to create a copy of an object, you have two options: shallow copy and deep copy. The main difference between the two is how they handle nested objects.\n\n**Shallow Copy (copy() method)**\n\nA shallow copy creates a new object and inserts references to the original elements. This means that if the original object contains mutable elements (like lists or dictionaries), the shallow copy will also contain references to the same elements. Any changes made to the original elements will be reflected in the shallow copy.\n\nHere's an example:\n```python\nimport copy\n\noriginal_list = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nshallow_copy = copy.copy(original_list)\n\nprint(shallow_copy)  # Output: [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n\n# Modify the original list\noriginal_list[0][0] = 10\n\nprint(shallow_copy)  # Output: [[10, 2], [3, 4]]\n```\nAs you can see, the shallow copy still references the same lists as the original, so changes to the original are reflected in the shallow copy.\n\n**Deep Copy (deepcopy() method)**\n\nA deep copy creates a new object and recursively adds copies of the child objects found in the original. This means that if the original object contains mutable elements, the deep copy will contain new, independent copies of those elements.\n\nHere's an example:\n```python\nimport copy\n\noriginal_list = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\ndeep_copy = copy.deepcopy(original_list)\n\nprint(deep_copy)  # Output: [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n\n# Modify the original list\noriginal_list[0][0] = 10\n\nprint(deep_copy)  # Output: [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n```\nAs you can see, the deep copy is independent of the original, so changes to the original do not affect the deep copy.\n\nIn summary:\n\n* Shallow copy (copy()) creates a new object with references to the original elements.\n* Deep copy (deepcopy()) creates a new object with independent copies of the child objects.\n\nWhen to use each:\n\n* Use shallow copy when you want to create a new object with the same structure as the original, but you don't need to modify the original elements.\n* Use deep copy when you want to create a new object with independent copies of the child objects, and you need to modify the original elements.", token_ids=[644, 13325, 11, 994, 499, 1390, 311, 1893, 264, 3048, 315, 459, 1665, 11, 499, 617, 1403, 2671, 25, 26682, 3048, 323, 5655, 3048, 13, 578, 1925, 6811, 1990, 279, 1403, 374, 1268, 814, 3790, 24997, 6302, 382, 334, 2059, 7331, 14882, 320, 8728, 368, 1749, 33395, 271, 32, 26682, 3048, 11705, 264, 502, 1665, 323, 50398, 15407, 311, 279, 4113, 5540, 13, 1115, 3445, 430, 422, 279, 4113, 1665, 5727, 26889, 5540, 320, 4908, 11725, 477, 58614, 705, 279, 26682, 3048, 690, 1101, 6782, 15407, 311, 279, 1890, 5540, 13, 5884, 4442, 1903, 311, 279, 4113, 5540, 690, 387, 27000, 304, 279, 26682, 3048, 382, 8586, 596, 459, 3187, 512, 74694, 12958, 198, 475, 3048, 271, 10090, 2062, 284, 4416, 16, 11, 220, 17, 1145, 510, 18, 11, 220, 19, 14623, 939, 7331, 16486, 284, 3048, 12511, 40909, 2062, 696, 1374, 24135, 7331, 16486, 8, 220, 674, 9442, 25, 4416, 16, 11, 220, 17, 1145, 510, 18, 11, 220, 19, 43550, 2, 50470, 279, 4113, 1160, 198, 10090, 2062, 58, 15, 1483, 15, 60, 