The government plays a role in regulating the system and providing subsidies to low-income individuals.\n\n**Access and Coverage**\n\n* France: All French citizens are covered by the public healthcare system, which provides comprehensive coverage for preventive care, hospitalization, and prescription medications. Private insurance is also available, but it is not mandatory.\n* Germany: All German citizens are required to have health insurance, which is typically provided through one of the sickness funds. The funds cover a wide range of services, including preventive care, hospitalization, and prescription medications.\n\n**Cost and Affordability**\n\n* France: The French healthcare system is generally considered to be more expensive than Germany's. However, the public system is designed to be more affordable, with a focus on preventive care and early intervention. Private insurance is also available, but it can be costly.\n* Germany: Germany's healthcare system is generally considered to be more affordable than France's. The sickness funds are non-profit organizations that pool risk and provide coverage to their members, which helps to keep costs low. However, there are some out-of-pocket costs for patients, such as copays and deductibles.\n\n**Outcomes and Performance**\n\n* France: France has a high life expectancy (82.5 years) and a low infant mortality rate (2.5 deaths per 1,000 live births). The country also has a high rate of healthcare access and utilization, with 95% of the population reporting good or very good health.\n* Germany: Germany has a high life expectancy (80.9 years) and a low infant mortality rate (3.4 deaths per 1,000 live births). The country also has a high rate of healthcare access and utilization,", token_ids=[334, 34587, 315, 39435, 15264, 304, 9822, 323, 10057, 57277, 50100, 323, 10057, 527, 1403, 7665, 5961, 449, 1664, 64868, 18985, 6067, 11, 1855, 449, 1202, 5016, 17910, 11, 36486, 11, 323, 11774, 13, 1115, 12593, 22262, 311, 11415, 27